  "knowledgePattern": "Use symmetric limits to cancel odd powers before integrating",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Use the two matching halves",
      "body": "<p>Sketch one odd-power term between −1 and 1. Compare its area above the axis with its area below.</p>",
      "recap": "Equal and opposite heights cancel in a signed integral.",
      "pitfall": "The constant term is an even-power term: it is a multiple of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow></math>.",
      "pause": "Which terms can you leave out of the integral?",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p2-b3-l04",
          "reminder": "Compare values at opposite inputs to check a function’s symmetry."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Integrate the terms that remain",
      "body": "<p>For each remaining term, compare its coefficient with its power. Apply the power rule and evaluate at 1 and −1.</p>",
      "recap": "To integrate a power, increase the power by 1 and divide by that new power.",
      "pitfall": "Count every remaining term, including the constant.",
      "pause": "Find each contribution and add them.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l20",
          "reminder": "Evaluate the antiderivative at the upper limit, then subtract its value at the lower limit."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> For every odd power, the contributions on the two sides of zero cancel. For an even power <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, the corresponding coefficient is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x0222B;</mo><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> The even powers are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>8</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>: six terms. Their total integral is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></math>. Answer C.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> Alternatively, an antiderivative of the entire integrand is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>11</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. Its values at 1 and −1 are 11 and −1, again giving 12.</p>"
